.NET 8+ ships with `System.TimeProvider` — no custom abstraction needed. Here's the pattern:

**Inject `TimeProvider` into your class:**

```csharp
public class ReportGenerator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider;

    public ReportGenerator(TimeProvider timeProvider)
    {
        _timeProvider = timeProvider;
    }

    public Report Generate()
    {
        var now = _timeProvider.GetUtcNow(); // instead of DateTime.UtcNow
        // ...
    }
}
```

**In production (DI registration):**

```csharp
services.AddSingleton(TimeProvider.System);
```

**In tests, use the built-in fake:**

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing; // from Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing NuGet

var fakeTime = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 7, 7, 12, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));

var generator = new ReportGenerator(fakeTime);
var report = generator.Generate();

Assert.Equal(new DateTime(2026, 7, 7, 12, 0, 0), report.GeneratedAt);

// You can also advance time:
fakeTime.Advance(TimeSpan.FromHours(1));
```

**Install the testing package:**

```
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing
```

This gives you deterministic time without writing any custom interfaces.
